{%- doc -%}
  Returns (echoes) the market-local URL to send a visitor to when they switch to
  `country`, keeping them on the page they are viewing wherever possible.

  Single source of truth for every country selector in the theme — the
  announcement bar, the mobile header selector, the menu drawer, the header
  icons dropdown and the footer all resolve their targets through here, so they
  cannot drift apart.

  The result is a **root-relative path** (`/products/x`, `/en-us/products/x`),
  never absolute. An absolute `{{ shop.url }}` target throws the visitor from
  whatever host they are on over to the live domain — which silently ejects
  anyone testing on a preview theme.

  Order of preference:
    1. the market's override metafield, for products listed separately per
       region (e.g. garda-hot-tub -> garda-hot-tub-etl)
    2. the current path with the current market root stripped, which resolves
       for any product or page published to the target market
    3. the market root, if we are already at the root

  Products carrying a region-specific listing rely on (1), or on a URL redirect
  from the shared handle to the regional one. Without one of those a product
  that is missing from the target market's catalog will bounce.

  Only markets with their own web presence (a /en-xx subfolder) get a locale
  prefix. A market with no web presence of its own — "Guernsey, Isle of Man,
  Jersey" is served from the primary domain alongside the UK — has no
  /en-gg, /en-je or /en-im path, and linking to one 404s (Semrush flagged 281
  such links on 2026-11-09). Liquid cannot see a market's web presence, so
  those markets carry the market metafield `routing.served_from_root = true`
  and resolve to the root path, exactly like the default market.

  @param {object} country - Entry from `localization.available_countries`
  @param {string} [eu_override] - Product URL path override for the EU market
  @param {string} [us_override] - Product URL path override for the US market
  @param {string} [uk_override] - Product URL path override for the UK market

  @example
  {%- capture market_url -%}
    {%- render 'localization-market-url', country: country, uk_override: uk_override -%}
  {%- endcapture -%}
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Markets with no web presence of their own (routing.served_from_root) live on
    the UK root and sell from its catalog, so they take the UK override even when
    their country.code says otherwise. "Guernsey, Isle of Man, Jersey" carries
    country.code EU, which sent it to /products/*-ce paths that only exist under
    EU subfolders (2026-11-10 broken-link report: /produits/spa-tahoe-ce).
  endcomment
  assign link_override = ''
  if country.market.metafields.routing.served_from_root == true
    assign link_override = uk_override
  elsif country.market.metafields.country.code == 'EU'
    assign link_override = eu_override
  elsif country.market.metafields.country.code == 'US'
    assign link_override = us_override
  elsif country.market.metafields.country.code == 'UK'
    assign link_override = uk_override
  endif

  comment
    Every market uses Shopify's lowercase locale slug (/en-us, /en-de), which is
    the canonical form and the one the hreflang tags advertise. Uppercase URLs
    still resolve, so older links keep working.
  endcomment
  assign country_slug = country.iso_code | downcase

  assign target_path = ''
  if link_override != blank
    comment
      Overrides are stored without a leading slash (products/x), but tolerate one
      so a hand-entered "/products/x" doesn't become //products/x.
    endcomment
    assign override_head = link_override | slice: 0
    if override_head == '/'
      assign link_override = link_override | slice: 1, 2000
    endif
    assign target_path = '/' | append: link_override
  else
    assign current_path = request.path
    assign market_root = routes.root_url

    comment
      Strip the market root case-insensitively. routes.root_url is the canonical
      lowercase form (/en-de), but visitors still arrive on the uppercase
      variant (/en-DE) from links, bookmarks and search results created before
      this selector was lowercased. A case-sensitive strip leaves the old root
      in place and stacks the new one on top: /en-us/en-DE/products/...
    endcomment
    if market_root != blank and market_root != '/'
      assign root_size = market_root | size
      assign root_lower = market_root | downcase
      assign path_head = current_path | slice: 0, root_size | downcase
      if path_head == root_lower
        assign current_path = current_path | slice: root_size, 2000
      endif
    endif

    unless current_path == '/' or current_path == blank
      assign target_path = current_path
    endunless
  endif

  comment
    Markets with their own subfolder carry their locale slug; the default
    market, and any market flagged `routing.served_from_root` (no web presence
    of its own), are served from the root. `target_path` is blank when we are
    already at the root, so fall back to '/' — an empty href resolves to the
    *current* URL, which would leave the visitor on the page without ever
    switching market.
  endcomment
  assign served_from_root = false
  if country.market.metafields.default.market == true
    assign served_from_root = true
  elsif country.market.metafields.routing.served_from_root == true
    assign served_from_root = true
  endif

  comment
    Keep the visitor's language only if the target market offers it. German and
    French are enabled in just a few markets (de: Germany, Switzerland; fr:
    France), so /de-ee or /fr-us do not exist — the 2026-11-10 broken-link
    report had 1,716 such links. Otherwise fall back to English, which every
    market has, or failing that to the market's first language.
  endcomment
  assign target_language = ''
  assign fallback_language = ''
  for language in country.available_languages
    if language.iso_code == localization.language.iso_code
      assign target_language = language
    endif
    if language.iso_code == 'en' or fallback_language == blank
      assign fallback_language = language
    endif
  endfor
  if target_language == blank
    assign target_language = fallback_language
  endif
  assign language_code = target_language.iso_code | default: localization.language.iso_code

  if served_from_root
    comment
      The root serves the primary language bare and the others under /de, /fr.
    endcomment
    assign market_url = target_path | default: '/'
    if target_language != blank and target_language.primary == false
      assign market_url = '/' | append: language_code | append: target_path
    endif
  else
    assign market_prefix = '/' | append: language_code | append: '-' | append: country_slug
    assign market_url = market_prefix | append: target_path
  endif

  echo market_url
-%}
